From f185f82f3ee8b27dba6974430de0f6acbce1bf8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:07:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] guilib: an animated image plays from its first frame each
 time it shows

An image that is not on screen is not processed, so its animated texture
does not move on while it is hidden. Kodi starts it again only where the
image frees its resources when it is hidden (an image of a window that
allocates them dynamically, hidden itself): an image in a group that is
hidden, or in a window that keeps its resources (the pointer window), went
on from the frame it was hidden on when it showed again, and an animation
that plays once and had ended did not play again.

The window manager now starts a pass of the GUI each time it processes the
windows, and each window counts passes of its own: one for each pass that
processes its controls, and one more when it was not shown in the pass of
the GUI before (closed, or another window was the active one). An image
that its window processes in two of its passes that do not follow each
other was not on screen in between, and its texture plays from its first
frame (CGUIProcessPass, GUIProcessPass.h; CGUITexture::RestartAnimation(),
which marks the image dirty where the frame changes). No time decides it:
a GUI that is not processed for a while (the display asleep, a change of
the display mode that holds the thread) starts no pass, and an image that
stayed on screen goes on. A window that smartredraw leaves as it is, the
same layout processed twice in a pass (a wrapping list), a modal loop
inside a window's processing and a control processed outside its window's
pass (the movers of the screen calibration) are no gap.

Estuary itself has no animated texture: this is for the animated GIF and
APNG images that add-ons and libraries show (the two that Kodi animates)
and for skins with animated images and pointers.

Tests (outside the tree): TestGUIProcessPass, a model of the GUI that drives
the real header (14 cases, 9 mutants that it catches), and a check of the
wiring that fails on the tree before. In QEMU, the image of the build
before with this Kodi: an animated GIF in a group of an add-on's window
that is hidden for 1.5 s shows its first frame again when it shows (the
build before: the frame that the animation had got to). The idea is from
YBLOD (kodi-9993).
